  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nBreezy conditions that we have seen today will briefly come to an \nend this evening as we lose daytime mixing, along with the easing of \nour pressure gradient. Temperatures overnight will stay in the 40s \nwith minimal cloud cover. As our jet stream moves to the north and \nforms more of a ridging pattern, along with a surface high to our \neast, unseasonably warm temperatures in the upper 60s/low 70s will \ncontinue on Thursday with lighter winds. Overnight conditions will \nbe similar to Wednesday night, with temperatures in the 40s, and \nminimal cloud cover.\n\nThe next disturbance we are watching is the trough that is currently \non the Pacific Coast as of Wednesday Afternoon. The trough will \ncontinue to dig into the Southwest region throughout Thursday and \nThursday Night, bringing the jet-max just to our west by Friday \nmorning. With the ridging over our area expected during the day, \ntemperatures will again climb up into the 70s. Gusty winds will \nreturn as a result of heating allowing for mixing, along with a \npressure gradient over the Plains associated with a surface low \ndeveloping over the Dakotas. The low lifts northward during the day \nSaturday, as continued ridging features allow for unseasonable \nwarmth.\n\nConditions change Saturday evening as a cold front associated with \nthe low begins to drag in our direction. This will be our forcing \nmechanism to see the return for precipitation come Saturday evening \nand night. Confidence in precipitation is in place, with the NBM \ngiving us high probability of seeing precipitation. Looking at \ntotals, the model also generates about a 50-60% chance of seeing \n0.25\" or more of total rainfall Saturday Night. As CAMs begin to \ncome in, rain and isolated thunderstorms form along the cold front \nto our west, and progress to the east, getting our area by Saturday \nevening. In terms of instability, overall positioning of the trough \nwill not allow for temperatures aloft to get cold enough, keeping \nCAPE totals below 500 J/kg. This will likely keep any storms that do \ndevelop below severe limits. Lack of instability has been the trend \nwith this system for the past few days, but monitoring of the event \nwill still continue as it comes closer.\n\nAs the cold front passes and the trough exits to the east, \ntemperatures Sunday will only get up into the upper 40s/low 50s, \nwith lows falling below freezing. This is associated with cold air \nadvection from the north, as winds will be from that direction. \nSimilar conditions will stick around to at least early next\nweek.",
